|passage=We presently turned just aside from the trail into an episode of beautiful prairie, one of a succession along the plateau at the crest of the range. At this height of about five thousand feet, the snows remain until June. In this fair, oval, forest-circled prairie of my '''nooning''', the grass was long and succulent, as if it grew in the bed of a drained lake.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1889|author=w:Mark Twain|title={{w|A Connecticut Yankee in King Arthur's Court}} Chapter XX|url=https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Connecticut_Yankee_in_King_Arthur's_Court/Chapter_XX
|passage=Between six and nine we made ten miles, which was plenty for a horse carrying triple—man, woman, and armor; then we stopped for a long '''nooning''' under some trees by a limpid brook.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1906|author=w:Andy Adams|title=The Double Trail
|passage=Well, we crossed and '''nooned''', lying around on purpose to give them a good lead, and when we hit the trail back in these sand-hills, there he was, not a mile ahead, and you can see there was no chance to get around}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992| title=All the Pretty Horses|author=Cormac McCarthy|isbn=9780330510936| page=157| passage= They '''nooned''' at a spring and squatted about the cold and blackened sticks of some former fire and ate cold beans and tortillas out of a newspaper. }}

