===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*nes-}}
{{ety|en|:bor|la-new:nostalgia<ety:af<grc:νόστος><grc:ἄλγος>:calque<de:Heimweh>>|tree=1}}
From {{bor|en|la-new|nostalgia}}, {{coin|en|Johannes Hofer|in=1688|nocap=1|w=-}} from {{der|en|grc|νόστος||returning home}} + {{m|grc|ἄλγος||pain}}, calquing {{cal|en|de|Heimweh|nocat=1|nocap=1|notext=1}}.<ref>{{cite-book|la|title=Dissertatio medica de nostalgia, oder Heimwehe|year=1688|publisher=Johann Jakob Harder|location=Basel|author=Johannes Hofer|doi=10.3931/e-rara-18924|passage=Neque verò denomine deliberanti convenientuis occurrit, remque explicandam præciſius deſignans, quam ''Noſtalgias'' vocabulum, origine græcum, & quidem duabus ex vocibus compoſitum, quorum alterum Νόστος Reditum in Patriam, alterum Ἄλγος dolorem aut triſtitiam ſignificat: {{...}}|t=<!-- Nor does it come to mind that I am considering a suitable name, and designating the matter to be explained more precisely, than the name ''Nostalgias'', a Greek in origin, composed of two words, one of which means Νόστος Return to Homeland, the other Ἄλγος pain or sadness: {{...}} -->}}</ref> {{cog|grc||*νοσταλγία}} is unattested. Transferred sense probably influenced by {{cog|fr|nostalgie}}, especially in literature.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

Compare {{cog|it|nostalgia}}, {{cog|es|nostalgia}}, {{cog|pt|nostalgia}} and {{cog|fr|nostalgie}}.

