===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of [[negating]] something.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Gessica Puccini|title=Lang Belta: the Belter language from SYFY/Amazon’s The Expanse|magazine=Lingoblog|url=https://www.lingoblog.dk/en/lang-belta-the-belter-language-from-syfy-amazons-the-expanse/|date=2021-10-05|passage=As an isolating language Belter Creole is rich in particles. Particles are used to indicate both '''negation''' and interrogation: ''na'' is the negative particle and it is placed before the verb|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520041529/https://www.lingoblog.dk/en/lang-belta-the-belter-language-from-syfy-amazons-the-expanse/|archivedate=20 May 2026}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A [[denial]] or [[contradiction]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1909|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=The Flirt's Tragedy
|passage=But it pleased her to play on my passion / And whet me to pleadings / That won from her mirthful '''negations''' / And scornings undue.}}
# {{lb|en|logic|linguistics|countable}} A [[proposition]] which is the [[contradictory]] of another proposition and which can be obtained from that other proposition by the appropriately placed addition, or insertion, of the word "not". (Or, in [[symbolic logic]], by prepending that proposition with the symbol for the logical operator "not".)
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=Mark Sainsbury|title=Logical Forms &mdash; An Introduction to Philosophical Logic| chapter=1| isbn=978-0-63121-679-7| section=&sect;4| page=19| edition=2nd| publisher=Blackwell Publishing| passage=You get the [[negation]] of a proposition if you insert "not" (or some equivalent expression) into it in such a way as to form a contradictory of it.}}
# {{lb|en|logic}} The logical operation which obtains such (negated) propositions.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2011-07-20|author=Edwin Mares| title=Propositional Functions| site=The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy| url=http://plato.stanford.edu/archives/fall2011/entries/propositional-function| accessdate=2012-07-15
|passage=Although some of the logicians working in term logic have very complicated treatments of [[negation]], we can see the origin of the modern conception in the extensional tradition as well. In Boole and most of his followers, the negation of a term is understood as the set theoretic complement of the class represented by that term. For this reason, the [[negation]] of classical propositional logic is often called ‘Boolean [[negation]]’.}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics|countable}} A [[negative]] [[particle]]; a [[word]], particularly an [[adverb]], with a negative [[meaning]]: for instance, ''no'' and ''not'' in English, ''nōn'', ''haud'' and ''nē'' in Latin, οὐ and μή in ancient Greek.

