===Etymology===
{{lbor|en|la|nimbus|t=dark cloud}}. {{doublet|en|nimb}}.

{{senseno|en|cloud|uc=1}} (“grey rain cloud”) was {{coinage|en|Luke Howard|w=Luke Howard (meteorologist)|nat=the British|occ=chemist and amateur meteorologist|nocap=1}} (1772–1864):<ref>{{cite-journal|en|author=[[w:Luke Howard (meteorologist)|Luke Howard]]|title=XVIII. On the Modifications of Clouds, and on the Principles of Their Production, Suspension, and Destruction; being the Substance of an Essay Read before the Askesian Society in the Session 1802–3.|editor=w:Alexander Tilloch|journal=[[w:Philosophical Magazine|The Philosophical Magazine and Journal: Comprehending the Various Branches of Science, the Liberal and Fine Arts, Agriculture, Manufactures, and Commerce]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} &#91;[[w:Richard Taylor (editor)|Richard Taylor]]&#93; for Alexander Tilloch; and sold by Messrs. Richardson,{{nb...|Cornhill; Cadell and Davies, Strand; Longman and Rees, Pater-noster Row; Debrett, Piccadilly; Murray, No. 32, Fleet street; Symonds, Pater-noster Row; Bell, No. 148, Oxford-street; Vernor and Hood, Poultry; Harding, No. 36, St. James’s-street; Bell and Bradfute, Edinburgh; Brash and Reid, Glasgow; and W. Gilbert, Dublin.}}|month=July|year=1803|volume=XVI|issue=62|page=106|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/s01philosophicalmag16londuoft/page/106/mode/1up|issn=1478-6435|oclc=1236055946|passage=[T]he cirri ''vegetate'', as it were, in proportion to the quantity of rain falling, and give the cloud a character by which it is easily known at great distances, and to which, in the language of meteorology, we may appropriate the nimbus of the Latins.}}</ref> see the 1803 quotation.

