===Noun===
{{en-noun|nimbi|+}}

# {{senseid|en|circle of light}} {{lb|en|art|heraldry}} A [[circle]] of [[light]]; a {{l|en|halo|id=painting}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Henry Wadsworth Longfellow|chapter=[[s:Flower-de-Luce (Collection)/Giotto's Tower|Giotto's Tower]]|title=Flower-de-Luce|year=1867|passage=Fail of the '''nimbus''' which the artists paint / Around the shining forehead of the saint}}
#* {{RQ:James American|passage=Several times, taking a walk from his inn into meadows and parks, he stopped by a well-worn stile, looked across through the early evening at a gray church tower, with its dusky '''nimbus''' of thick-circling swallows, and remembered that this might have been part of the entertainment of his honeymoon.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|passage=“Don’t ye be nervous, my dear good soul,” expostulated, between his coughs, a young man with a wet face and his straw hat so far back upon his head that the brim encircled it like the '''nimbus''' of a saint.}}
#* {{RQ:London Faith of Men|page=102|passage=A '''nimbus''' of mosquitoes buzzed about each man’s head.}}
#* {{RQ:Conrad Within the Tides|page=108|passage=Just as the rising sun threw its golden '''nimbus''' around the purple shape of the headland, the Planter of Malata was perceived pacing bare-headed the curve of the little bay.}}
# {{senseid|en|cloud}}{{lb|en|meteorology}} A [[gray#Adjective|grey]] [[rain cloud]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1803|month=July|author=[[w:Luke Howard (meteorologist)|Luke Howard]]|title=XVIII. On the Modifications of Clouds, and on the Principles of Their Production, Suspension, and Destruction; being the Substance of an Essay Read before the Askesian Society in the Session 1802–3.|editor=w:Alexander Tilloch|journal=[[w:Philosophical Magazine|The Philosophical Magazine and Journal: Comprehending the Various Branches of Science, the Liberal and Fine Arts, Agriculture, Manufactures, and Commerce]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} &#91;[[w:Richard Taylor (editor)|Richard Taylor]]&#93; for Alexander Tilloch; and sold by Messrs. Richardson,{{nb...|Cornhill; Cadell and Davies, Strand; Longman and Rees, Pater-noster Row; Debrett, Piccadilly; Murray, No. 32, Fleet street; Symonds, Pater-noster Row; Bell, No. 148, Oxford-street; Vernor and Hood, Poultry; Harding, No. 36, St. James’s-street; Bell and Bradfute, Edinburgh; Brash and Reid, Glasgow; and W. Gilbert, Dublin.}}|volume=XVI|issue=62|pages=99–100 and 107|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/s01philosophicalmag16londuoft/page/97/mode/1up|issn=1478-6435|oclc=1236055946|passage={{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/s01philosophicalmag16londuoft/page/97/mode/1up pages 99–100]}} {{smallcaps|Cumulo-cirro-stratus}} vel '''{{smallcaps|Nimbus}}'''. {{...}} The rain cloud. A cloud or system of clouds from which rain is falling. It is a horizontal sheet, above which the cirrus spreads, while the cumulus enters it laterally and from beneath. {{...}} {{quote-gloss|[https://archive.org/details/s01philosophicalmag16londuoft/page/107/mode/1up page 107]}} The '''nimbus''', although in itself one of the least beautiful clouds, is yet now and then superbly decorated with its attendant the rainbow; which can only be seen in perfection when backed by the widely extended uniform gloom of this modification.}}

====Usage notes====
* {{sense|circle of light}} In [[heraldry]] and [[art]], a nimbus may be considered distinct from a halo. The term {{m|en|halo}} can refer to an open ring of light or of gold behind the head of a [[saint]], while {{m|en||nimbus}} refers to a solid disk of light or gold.

