===Etymology 2===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*melh₂-|id1=grind|*(s)meld-}}
From {{affix|en|mull|t1=to mix (clay and sand) under a roller to prepare a mould|-er|pos2={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|agent noun}}s}}.<ref>Compare {{R:Collins English Dictionary|entry=mull|pos=v}}</ref> {{m|en||Mull}} is possibly derived from {{m|en|mull|t=(''chiefly Northern England'') to grind to powder, crumble, powder, pulverize}}, from {{inh|en|enm|mollen}}, {{m|enm|mullen|t=to moisten (something); to soften (something) by making wet; to become liquid; to drizzle; to crumble or soften (something) by grinding; to fondle or pet (something)}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=mollen|pos=v|id=MED28503}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|moillier}}, {{m|fro|muillier|t=to make wet}} (modern {{cog|fr|mouiller}}), and from its {{glossary|etymon}} {{der|en|la-vul|*molliāre}}, {{m|la-vul|*mulliāre|t=to make wet}}, from {{der|en|la|molliāre}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|molliō|t=to soften}}, from {{der|en|la|mollis|t=soft}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)meld-|t=to melt; to soften}} or {{m|ine-pro|*melh₂-|id=grind|t=to crush, grind}}) + {{m|la|-iō|pos={{glossary|suffix}} forming {{glossary|factitive}} {{glossary|verb}}s from {{glossary|adjective}}s}}.

